import BaseLayout from '../layouts/BaseLayout.astro';
import GPryExplorer from '../components/GPryExplorer.astro';
const schema={"@context":"https://schema.org","@type":"SoftwareSourceCode","name":"GPry","description":"Gaussian-process surrogate modelling and active learning for Bayesian inference with computationally expensive likelihoods.","codeRepository":"https://github.com/jonaselgammal/GPry","programmingLanguage":"Python","author":{"@id":"https://jonaselgammal.github.io/#jonas-el-gammal"}};
---
<BaseLayout title="GPry — Bayesian inference for expensive likelihoods" description="GPry uses Gaussian-process surrogate modelling and active learning to accelerate Bayesian inference with expensive likelihoods." schema={schema}>
  <header class="page-head"><div class="shell"><span class="eyebrow">Open-source scientific ML</span><h1>GPry</h1><p>Bayesian inference for expensive likelihoods, using Gaussian-process surrogates and active learning to spend evaluations where they matter.</p><div class="actions"><a class="button button--solid" href="https://gpry.readthedocs.io/en/latest/">Documentation</a><a class="button" href="https://github.com/jonaselgammal/GPry">Source code</a><a class="button" href="https://pypi.org/project/gpry/">PyPI</a></div></div></header>
  <article class="prose software-copy"><img class="gpry-gif" src="/assets/img/gpry_animation.gif" alt="Animated GPry active-learning run"/><h2>What problem does it solve?</h2><p>Conventional Monte Carlo methods can require many evaluations of a likelihood or posterior. When each call runs a cosmological solver or waveform model, that quickly becomes prohibitive. GPry learns a surrogate of the log-posterior and actively selects the next expensive evaluations.</p><pre><code>python -m pip install gpry</code></pre><h2>When is it useful?</h2><p>GPry is aimed at deterministic, sufficiently smooth likelihoods in low- to moderate-dimensional problems where evaluations are expensive. It is not a universal replacement for MCMC or nested sampling: stochastic likelihoods, very high dimensions, and widely separated modes require additional care.</p><h2>Primary references</h2><ol><li><a href="https://arxiv.org/abs/2211.02045">Fast and Robust Bayesian Inference using Gaussian Processes with GPry</a></li><li><a href="https://arxiv.org/abs/2305.19267">Parallelized Acquisition for Active Learning using Monte Carlo Sampling</a></li><li><a href="https://arxiv.org/abs/2503.21871">Accelerating LISA inference with Gaussian processes</a></li></ol></article>
  <section class="shell demo"><GPryExplorer /></section>
</BaseLayout>
<style>.actions{display:flex;flex-wrap:wrap;gap:.7rem;margin-top:2rem}.software-copy{padding-top:4rem}.gpry-gif{background:white;border:1px solid var(--line);margin-bottom:3rem;width:100%}.demo{margin-top:5rem}</style>
